:name: CANcestry T2 virtual bench - CAN fault scenarios
:description: Bring-up of the STM32G474-class platform model plus the CAN bus fault injector for the H-10 Bus-Off / CRC scenarios (issue #62).

# CANcestry T2 virtual bench - CAN fault scenario script (H-10, issue #62).
#
# Implements: HW-FR-003 (ISO 11898-2 CAN interfaces: the Bus-Off recovery
# sequence and CRC error handling of the gateway node are exercised on the
# virtual bench against the REAL v1.0.0 firmware). Governing documents:
# docs/hw/virtual-bench-plan.md sections 1, 3 and 6;
# docs/hw/tool-qualification.md section 3 (can_fault_injector, TCL1) and
# section 4.5; HwAGENTS.md rules 1, 5 and 13.
#
# WHY A SECOND .resc (issue #62 constraint 4, "extend, don't modify"): the
# H-07/H-08 retention bring-up script cancestry-hw.resc is left byte-identical.
# It is the artifact 25 CI dispatches qualified, its QA-EV-01 symbol-hook
# contract belongs to the retention scenario, and the fault scenarios need a
# different ELF (the off-tree CAN fault driver) plus different hooks. This
# script loads the SAME unmodified platform description
# (stm32g474-cancestry.repl) and the SAME four scripted register models, and
# ADDS the CAN fault injector plus the fault-event capture hooks.
#
# Driven by hw/virtual-bench/scenarios/t2_busoff_001.py and
# hw/virtual-bench/scenarios/t2_crc_001.py (via hw/virtual-bench/
# t2_fault_common.py):
#
#   $elf   path of the fault-scenario firmware ELF (mandatory; built off-tree
#          from tag v1.0.0 by hw/virtual-bench/firmware/build_firmware.sh with
#          CANCESTRY_T2_DRIVER=can_fault). Set by the runner as a QUOTED path
#          with NO '@' marker (bring-up finding F-32: LoadELF validates the raw
#          string with File.Exists); the guard below fails closed when it is
#          missing.
#   $t2_seed  fixed deterministic seed, unquoted (bring-up finding F-31:
#          `emulation SetSeed` binds to SetSeed(int) and the monitor accepts
#          only DecimalIntegerToken for int parameters). Must stay equal to
#          fmi_bridge.FIXED_SEED.
#
# Determinism (HwAGENTS.md rule 5): the machine is left PAUSED and is advanced
# exclusively by the orchestrator's fixed-size `emulation RunFor` quanta
# (100 us master step, the H-07 bridge contract). No wall clock is consumed.

using sysbus

$t2_seed?=123456789

# Fail-closed guard: T2 executes a REAL v1.0.0 ELF (HW-PLAN section 8.3); the
# bench never falls back to a reimplementation of the firmware.
python "assert variables['elf'] not in ('', None), 'cancestry-hw-fault.resc: no firmware ELF provided (set $elf before including this script)'"
python "import Antmicro.Renode.Logging as _clog"
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step0: python logger channel OK, elf=%s' % variables['elf'])"

mach create "cancestry"
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step2: machine created')"

# Same platform description as the retention bring-up (unmodified file).
# $ORIGIN-absolute path: bring-up finding F-24 (the process CWD of a headless
# Renode is the install directory, never this script's directory).
machine LoadPlatformDescription $ORIGIN/stm32g474-cancestry.repl
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step3: platform loaded')"

# The four scripted register models of the H-07 platform, registered with
# $ORIGIN-absolute paths and QUOTED names (bring-up findings F-28 and F-30:
# PythonPeripheral resolves a bare `filename:` against the process CWD, and
# PyDevFromFile's `name` parameter is a string, which the monitor binds only
# from a StringToken/PathToken). The fault driver arms the IWDG with the same
# register sequence as the retention driver and reads the reset cause from
# RCC_CSR, so all four stay registered - unchanged.
machine PyDevFromFile $ORIGIN/iwdg_model.py 0x40003000 0x14 true "iwdg"
machine PyDevFromFile $ORIGIN/rtc_backup_model.py 0x40002800 0x64 false "rtc_backup"
machine PyDevFromFile $ORIGIN/gpioa_model.py 0x48000000 0x400 true "gpioa"
machine PyDevFromFile $ORIGIN/rcc_model.py 0x40021000 0x98 true "rcc"
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step3c: 4 register models registered')"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# H-10 EXTENSION: the CAN bus fault injector (issue #62 deliverable 1).
#
# Registered HERE rather than declared in stm32g474-cancestry.repl for the
# F-28 reason (a `filename:` attribute in a .repl is resolved CWD-relative
# inside the PythonPeripheral constructor, which breaks headless CI) and to
# keep the platform description file itself unchanged.
#
# Base 0x40000000 is the address the issue names. It is an APB1 address that
# the v1.0.0 firmware never accesses (hal_stm32.c touches only DWT/DEMCR, the
# FDCAN bases 0x40006400/0x40006800, and - through watchdog.c - IWDG, RTC/BKP
# and RCC), and the platform model declares no peripheral there, so the
# registration neither overlaps an existing model nor changes firmware-visible
# behavior. Size 0x400 is one guest page (bring-up finding F-29).
machine PyDevFromFile $ORIGIN/can_fault_injector.py 0x40000000 0x400 true "can_fault_injector"
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step3d: can_fault_injector registered at 0x40000000')"

# Deterministic execution: fixed global quantum (the 100 us master step of the
# H-07 bridge contract) and the fixed seed for the only Renode randomness
# source. `emulation RunFor` advances virtual time exactly.
emulation SetGlobalQuantum "0.0001"
emulation SetSeed $t2_seed
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step4: quantum and seed set')"

sysbus LoadELF $elf
# Readback: the initial SP word of the vector table (0x20018000 per
# firmware/startup.s) proves the ELF segments reached flash.
python "bus = sysbus if 'sysbus' in dir() else (machine['sysbus'] if 'machine' in dir() else monitor.Machine['sysbus']); v = bus.ReadDoubleWord(0x08000000); _clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step5: vector SP=0x%08x (ELF loaded)' % v)"

# Trace-area magic: the H-07 contract word (the orchestrator refuses to run
# against a machine whose capture area was not initialized by a bring-up
# script) plus the H-10 fault-capture area marker.
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x60000000 0x54324353
sysbus WriteDoubleWord 0x60000040 0x00000000
python "bus = monitor.Machine['sysbus']; _clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step7: t2_trace readback=0x%08x (expected 0x54324353)' % bus.ReadDoubleWord(0x60000000))"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fault-event capture (HwAGENTS.md rule 5: deterministic, hashed trace).
#
# Each symbol hook stamps the exact emulation virtual time (microseconds) of
# the REAL firmware entering the named function - nothing in the firmware is
# modified or reimplemented. The hooked names live in the OFF-TREE T2 driver
# (hw/virtual-bench/firmware/can_fault.c); each one wraps a v1.0.0 call:
#
#   t2_can_busoff_detect   the poll pass that observed PSR.BO and entered
#                          cancestry_hal_raise_fault(CANCESTRY_HAL_FAULT_BUS_OFF)
#                          -> CANCESTRY_HAL_IF_STATE_BUS_OFF + FAULT_RAISED
#   t2_can_busoff_recover  the recovery action that clears CCCR.INIT, the
#                          ISO 11898-1 / RM0440 Bus-Off recovery request
#   t2_can_crc_detect      the poll pass that observed PSR.LEC = CRC error and
#                          raised the fault into the real queue
#
# The v1.0.0 ELF ships no CAN error interrupt handler (the platform HAL is
# poll-driven and the T2 driver installs no device IRQ), so the issue's
# "symbol hook on the CAN error interrupt handler" is realized as a hook on
# the firmware's error-detection function - the documented deviation is
# recorded in the scenario evidence and in tool-qualification.md section 4.5.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
cpu AddSymbolHook "t2_can_busoff_detect" """
machine['sysbus'].WriteDoubleWord(0x60000058, int(round(machine.ElapsedVirtualTime.TimeElapsed.TotalSeconds * 1000000)))
"""
cpu AddSymbolHook "t2_can_busoff_recover" """
machine['sysbus'].WriteDoubleWord(0x6000005c, int(round(machine.ElapsedVirtualTime.TimeElapsed.TotalSeconds * 1000000)))
"""
cpu AddSymbolHook "t2_can_crc_detect" """
machine['sysbus'].WriteDoubleWord(0x60000060, int(round(machine.ElapsedVirtualTime.TimeElapsed.TotalSeconds * 1000000)))
"""
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step6: fault symbol hooks installed')"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bus write hooks on the UNCHANGED fdcan1_scratch memory: they carry the
# firmware's own register writes into the medium without the injector ever
# having to guess them, and without modifying the existing peripheral
# (issue #62 constraint 4).
#
#   CCCR (0x40006418, offset 0x018): the ISO 11898-1 / RM0440 Bus-Off recovery
#     request is software clearing CCCR.INIT. The hook stamps the first such
#     transition into 0x60000048 (recovery_request_us); the injector releases
#     the medium 128 * 11 recessive bits later (704 us at the HW-FR-003
#     nominal 2 Mbit/s).
#   IR (0x40006450, offset 0x050): the error-logging acknowledge. The hook
#     stamps the first write that clears IR.ELO into 0x60000054 (crc_ack_us).
#
# Hook scope (src/Emulator/Extensions/Hooks/
# BusPeripheralsHooksPythonEngine.cs, v1.16.1): `self` (the hooked
# peripheral), `sysbus`, `machine`, `value`, `offset`. A before-write hook
# returns the script's final `value`, so these scripts leave it untouched -
# they observe, they never alter what the firmware wrote.
#
# Both hooks are first-event-only (guarded on the slot still reading 0) and
# touch only the plain t2_trace memory, so no hook ever re-enters a scripted
# peripheral.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
sysbus SetHookBeforePeripheralWrite machine['fdcan1_scratch'] """
if offset == 0x18 and (machine['sysbus'].ReadDoubleWord(0x60000048) == 0) and ((machine['sysbus'].ReadDoubleWord(0x40006418) & 1) == 1) and ((value & 1) == 0):
    machine['sysbus'].WriteDoubleWord(0x60000048, int(round(machine.ElapsedVirtualTime.TimeElapsed.TotalSeconds * 1000000)))
"""
sysbus SetHookBeforePeripheralWrite machine['fdcan1_scratch'] """
if offset == 0x50 and (machine['sysbus'].ReadDoubleWord(0x60000054) == 0) and ((machine['sysbus'].ReadDoubleWord(0x40006450) & 0x10000) != 0) and ((value & 0x10000) == 0):
    machine['sysbus'].WriteDoubleWord(0x60000054, int(round(machine.ElapsedVirtualTime.TimeElapsed.TotalSeconds * 1000000)))
"""
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step6b: FDCAN1 CCCR/IR write hooks installed')"

# Injector preflight readback (console-logged evidence that the peripheral
# loaded and its command interface answers): magic 0x43464931 ("CFI1"), bus
# state 0 (active), counters 0. The orchestrator asserts the same values over
# the monitor and fails closed on any mismatch.
python "bus = monitor.Machine['sysbus']; _clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step8: injector window magic=0x%08x (expected 0x43464931) state=%d (expected 0) busoff_count=%d crc_count=%d' % (bus.ReadDoubleWord(0x40000000), bus.ReadDoubleWord(0x40000010), bus.ReadDoubleWord(0x40000008), bus.ReadDoubleWord(0x4000000C)))"
python "_clog.Logger.Info('[cancestry-hw-fault] step9: include complete, machine left paused')"

# The machine is left PAUSED: the orchestrator alone advances emulation with
# fixed-size `emulation RunFor` quanta. This configuration must NOT be started
# with `start`.
